description:
In Elixir a module name is an atom and can be passed around and called through (`m.compare(a, b)`). In fz a module name in value position is unresolved:

    defmodule Cmp do
      def compare(a, b), do: Kernel.compare(a, b)
    end
    def via(m, a, b), do: m.compare(a, b)
    def main(), do: dbg(via(Cmp, 1, 2))

fz: "compiler2 lowering found unresolved value `Cmp`". Elixir prints :lt.

This is a language feature, not a library one: a module name as a value, and a remote call whose module is only known at runtime (resolved through the analysis where the value is known; a genuinely unknown module needs a runtime lookup or a loud refusal). Investigate before designing: how fz represents modules today, what the concordant ticket fz-sc8.25 (one module resolution for qualified calls) changes, and how this interacts with protocols.

Needed by the Enum extremes ticket for the module-sorter form (`Enum.max(dates, Date)`). Not needed by the distiller.
